# CoreMark 1.0 -- provenance of the vendored source tree, and the MD5
# fingerprints of the files whose modification the run rules forbid.
#
# PROVENANCE (sdk/apps/coremark/ is untracked, per sdk/.gitignore `/apps/*/`;
# this file is how it is re-obtained and re-verified):
#
#     git clone https://github.com/czietz/coremark.git sdk/apps/coremark
#     git -C sdk/apps/coremark checkout b546802
#
# commit b546802 = b54680215e981456ad80fb143d096c28c4b03e11 ("Fix a few
# typos"), Christian Zietz' fork of https://github.com/eembc/coremark carrying
# the Atari/TOS port in atari/. Our own port directory is NOT in that tree --
# it lives at sdk/overlays/coremark/newtos/ and is compiled from there.
#
# WHY THIS FILE AND NOT `make check`
# CoreMark ships a coremark.md5 and a `make check` target for exactly this
# purpose, but the shipped list is stale upstream metadata: it still carries
# the pre-2018 MD5s of core_list_join.c / core_main.c / core_state.c, so
# `make check` mismatches even on a completely untouched tree. (eembc's own
# HEAD fails it too, for coremark.h.) So the fingerprints below were pinned
# after comparing the vendored tree against eembc/coremark HEAD on 2026-09-04:
#
#   core_main.c, core_matrix.c, core_state.c, core_util.c -- byte-identical to
#     eembc HEAD, and their MD5s equal the values in eembc's own coremark.md5.
#   coremark.h -- equal to the value in BOTH coremark.md5 files; eembc's HEAD
#     file has since changed without their coremark.md5 being regenerated.
#   core_list_join.c -- one upstream revision older than eembc HEAD. The whole
#     textual delta is at core_bench_list():
#         eembc HEAD  :  list_data  info = {0};
#         this tree   :  list_data  info;
#     Both fields of `info` are assigned before any read (info.idx at the top of
#     the function, info.data16 as the first statement of the find loop, which
#     runs find_num = seed3 = 0x66 > 0 times), so the initializer is a
#     warning-cleanup with no reachable effect on the CRCs or the score.
#
# sdk/apps/build-coremark.sh verifies every line below before it compiles
# anything and refuses to build on a mismatch, so "we only touched the port
# directory" is a checked fact.
#
# Format: <md5>  <path relative to sdk/apps/coremark/>
761e73db06e1e9fbc7c322784f5def4c  core_list_join.c
4a9e6dadce1ac3866381021fbe843fc9  core_main.c
5fa21a0f7c3964167c9691db531ca652  core_matrix.c
fb49e7605c125306575a83f14f5798ac  core_state.c
45540ba2145adea1ec7ea2c72a1fbbcb  core_util.c
8ca974c013b380dc7f0d6d1afb76eb2d  coremark.h
